at were  covered in like one course in school  and just not nearly nearly nearly enough  to understand the whole process um so i  applied to several firms  and i got an offer from two firms um one  of them was a larger firm with bigger  projects and they offered me not a lot  but a little bit a little bit more money  which was a lot for me at that time  and the other firm was a smaller firm  only three people with smaller projects  and they were kind of i guess total  opposite  and as a young grad i was obviously more  tempted to take the job from the larger  firm you know they have more people they  have more resources and it's a little  cooler it's a little fancier but i just  asked around a lot from some of the  older grads  and i ended up taking the job from the  smaller firm for a little bit less money  and one of the deciding factors was  actually during the interview at the  small firm  one of the partners he couldn't make it  and so  there was an intern that actually took  part in the interview process which kind  of made an impression on me  he said both the partners are really  invested in is learning and there's  really good work-life balance and they  really help you get your hours so i  ended up going there and luckily it  turned out to be a good fit and i know  that i'm lucky that this doesn't happen  all the time i and i learned so much  during my three years there  and they really helped me get my  architecture license within three years  which is like pretty much the shortest  amount of time you can take which  obviously results in  ta-da more money  this lesson is it responds to another  common question which is how do i know  what type of firm i should work for and  it's actually a really great question  and something you should give a bit of  thought to  because early in your career  the firm that you work at it is gonna  kind of shape you like who you become as  an architect and the trajectory of your  career so  my advice here would be  try to 