 remotely and just like having a nice  time this is of course when cobit  happened and this was a pretty big hit  for my client and most of their projects  ended up getting cancelled and of course  my yoga retreat got cancelled as well at  that point i didn't have a job  and nobody was hiring at that time this  was really  scary for me because i kind of felt like  i'd made a humongous mistake like i quit  a good stable job at a good firm  and i just had a lot of anxiety  and um i also had a lot of time not  great combinations that's when i started  thinking about what could i do to make  money while using my skill sets and  interests of course my skill is  designing storytelling and being an  architect and at that time the youtube  algorithm probably because a lot of you  were having the same problems with me it  fed me this video by ali abdal talking  about how he was able to make money from  youtube that was when i made my first  video about the design process and if  you want to check it out i'll leave it  right here that's when i started my  youtube channel some of the videos  ended up getting a lot of traction and  the thing that eventually landed me  another job as an architect at another  firm and the lesson i learned from this  experience is that  when you don't know what to do  just go back to your foundations i say  this because when i was starting the  youtube channel the advice that was  given to me like over and over and over  again was to not make videos about  architecture and to make videos about  something else that would interest the  largest and widest broadest audience  possible when i really sat down and like  tried to list out the things that i was  interested in and the things that i was  good at i  just  couldn't  get myself to make videos about food or  fashion  or productivity so  i just made videos about design and  architecture and somehow it found an  audience and i think if you sit down and  look at your existing skills and your  interest you can have something that's  unique and you end up creating your own  niche i think in that first year of  kovid and i think this is probably a  pretty common experience that first year  i learned so much about myself like what  i liked and what i didn't like and what  my values were and what i was good at so  like i was saying before a couple months  into the pandemic in september of 2020 i  actually landed a job that specialized  in interior design tenant improvements  interior fit outs and i got brought on  as the architect to help like navigate  the building code and zoning bylaws and  you know all the legal stuff and also to  help out on some of their more complex  projects and professionally i thought  this was a great move for me  because you know i could start to build  a niche in this area um especially  because most of my previous projects had  been smaller projects and renovations  this was partially motivated by my fear  of running out of time and dying  i'm gonna get to a point where i you  know need to have kids and have a family  and where i can't focus on my career as  much and so i always thought it's better  to start building a niche early so  that was my thinking at the time but the  more i was there i kept feeling like  maybe i'd picked a specialty a little  too early in my career i'd only been  working in the field for like three four  years which is like totally an infant in  this industry and ultimately i i felt  like this was a time for me to be like  trying out different projects of  different scales and be learning from  more senior architects so that was a  massive  miscalculation on my part and after  about half a year i ended up leaving  that firm which was really hard because  they'd kind of made this role for me  there and  i built really good relationships with a  lot of the people and i just ended up  disappointing a lot of people there but  again this was another time when i  really learned a lot about myself first  that i real