ture  it was just the first time i i learned  why things are beautiful  and you know it is pretty mind-blowing  you you learn that every object and  every space you've come across has  meaning and there's like poetry behind  it  and there's like intent behind concrete  or stone or wood  and like learning about the idea of  phenomenology and it's all kind of like  all of a sudden you have this new  language and  now you understand and you can express  the world in a completely different way  so  yeah first year was very exciting but  my  excitement did not really translate into  good projects or good grades i was  actually pretty behind a lot of the  other students because a lot of them  actually knew that they wanted to go  into architecture pretty early on  and so when they were in high school  they were taking like drafting classes  and some of them already knew how to use  autocad and like 3d modeling  they didn't they knew what a plan and  section was which i didn't  needless to say  my grades were not  the best i kind of had to learn like all  of this new software on my free time and  it took me a painfully long time to do  things and i was just very lost and  confused and i had so many moments where  i really really wanted to drop out but  even when i thought it was never gonna  get better and even when i thought i was  like the worst worst worst student ever  things did start to take a turn  and i think the lesson here is  pain and reflection equals growth this  is something i learned from the book  principles by ray dalio if you don't  know him he's the founder of like the  biggest hedge fund in the world  bridgewater and with like 150 billion  dollars in assets and he basically  attributes his success to being able to  look at the world in terms of systems  and principles and one of these  principles is  yeah pain plus reflection  equals progress for me too  the first couple of years were really  really painful  but  when i look back in terms of my growth  