u're right if you wanted  to you could get these meta skills by  doing an online course or or even from  working for a firm right but  if you want to be an architect right now  these are hard requirements you can see  my video where i describe the  step-by-step process to become a  licensed architect and i think sometimes  because it's a hard requirement it's  really easy to fall into the trap of  thinking that this is just something you  have to get through and i think that  mindset can hinder you from fully taking  advantage of your education i think it's  a really subtle shift in mindset but  when you  think about your education as a  long-term goal and a long-term  investment in yourself  you're going to be asking more questions  you're going to be actively engaging  your profs and you're gonna be  reflecting on your process and like  trying to improve whenever you make a  mistake trying to understand why you got  a bad grade and trying to figure out why  certain things worked and like why  certain things didn't work and if  there's one thing we should be investing  in  it is our education and so the lesson  from this would be see education as a  long-term investment in yourself not a  requirement  and use this time to start building  systems for your workflow so that i can  benefit you down the road okay so i'm  officially through the school phase of  my life  but if you have any other questions  about that feel free to ask me in the  comments and i'll make sure to answer  them how do i decide what firm to work  for so once i finished my masters i felt  like i still didn't really know anything  about being an architect so my goal was  to fill in those gaps in my knowledge as  fast as possible and i really wanted to  learn how to navigate around code and  zoning i wanted to learn how to build  and the construction process  and how to work with contractors and  clients and i also wanted to learn how  an architecture business works and  basically all the things that were  covered in like one course in school  and just not nearly nearly nearly enough  to understand the whole process um so i  applied to several firms  and i got an offer from two firms um one  of them was a larger firm with bigger  projects and they offered me not a lot  but a little bit a little bit more money  which was a lot for me at that time  and the other firm was a smaller firm  only three people with smaller projects  and they were kind of i guess total  opposite  and as a young grad i was obviously more  tempted to take the job from the larger  firm you know they have more people they  have more resources and it's a little  cooler it's a little fancier but i just  asked around a lot from some of the  older grads  and i ended up taking the job from the  smaller firm for a little bit less money  and one of the deciding factors was  actually during the interview at the  small firm  one of the partners he couldn't make it  and so  there was an intern that actually took  part in the interview process which kind  of made an impression on me  he said both the partners are really  invested in is learning and there's  really good work-life balance and they  really help you get your hours so i  ended up going there and luckily it  turned out to be a good fit and i know  that i'm lucky that this doesn't happen  all the time i and i learned so much  during my three years there  and they really helped me get my  architecture license within three years  which is like pretty much the shortest  amount of time you can take which  obviously results in  ta-da more money  this lesson is it responds to another  common question which is how do i know  what type of firm i should work for and  it's actually a really great question  and something you should give a bit of  thought to  because early in your career  the firm that you work at it is gonna  kind of shape you like who you become as  an architect and the trajectory of your  career so  my advice here would be  try to 