tally new  perspective on things and i i would  highly highly highly recommend this book  for all of you if you haven't read it  she says enthusiasm is common and  endurance is rare and i think it's  really easy to say okay this is too hard  i'm done with this thing and i'm gonna  go to this new shiny thing but she says  true happiness and fulfillment comes  from getting really good at something  and slowly becoming an expert in things  learning new things is hard  and making mistakes is really hard and  being an intern and working on an intern  salary that's really hard it was really  hard for me  but once you gain more experience  things become easier and for me i really  started to love my job so this is the  next lesson which is like stay  persistent and don't give up and try to  tune out the negativity because i find  that in general unhappy people are the  most vocal and they're the ones who  complain the most well in general like  happy and satisfied people you know just  continue living their lives so  a little while after i finished my  requirements this was in march 2020 i  applied for my license and i ended up  leaving the firm that i was at i was  actually moonlighting while i was  working at this firm and my client was  the owner of this franchise bakery that  was growing really really fast  and i'll make a video on this and they  had plans to expand all over the world  it's the  type of work that's not really glamorous  and but it's also not a lot of work  it's not as exciting because they kind  of have their own branding and they want  you to basically replicate like similar  designs for each location yeah it's  relatively easy it's good money  and you can work on your own time and  you know at that time like since i  finished all my requirements i just  wanted to take a break and think about  what i want to do next and so i booked a  yoga retreat in mexico  that i've been wanting to go on for a  really long time  and i was planning on doing the work  remotely and just like having a nice  time this is of course when cobit  happened and this was a pretty big hit  for my client and most of their projects  ended up getting cancelled and of course  my yoga retreat got cancelled as well at  that point i didn't have a job  and nobody was hiring at that time this  was really  scary for me because i kind of felt like  i'd made a humongous mistake like i quit  a good stable job at a good firm  and i just had a lot of anxiety  and um i also had a lot of time not  great combinations that's when i started  thinking about what could i do to make  money while using my skill sets and  interests of course my skill is  designing storytelling and being an  architect and at that time the youtube  algorithm probably because a lot of you  were having the same problems with me it  fed me this video by ali abdal talking  about how he was able to make money from  youtube that was when i made my first  video about the design process and if  you want to check it out i'll leave it  right here that's when i started my  youtube channel some of the videos  ended up getting a lot of traction and  the thing that eventually landed me  another job as an architect at another  firm and the lesson i learned from this  experience is that  when you don't know what to do  just go back to your foundations i say  this because when i was starting the  youtube channel the advice that was  given to me like over and over and over  again was to not make videos about  architecture and to make videos about  something else that would interest the  largest and widest broadest audience  possible when i really sat down and like  tried to list out the things that i was  interested in and the things that i was  good at i  just  couldn't  get myself to make videos about food or  fashion  or productivity so  i just made videos about design and  architecture and somehow it found an  audience and i think if you sit down and  look at your existing skills and your  interest you can have something that's  unique and you end up creating your own  niche i think in that first year of  kovid and i think this is probably a  pretty common experience that first year  i learned so much about myself like what  i liked and what i didn't like and what  my values were and what i was good at so  like i was saying before a couple months  into the pandemic in september of 2020 i  actually landed a job that specialized  in interior design tenant improvements  interior fit outs and i got brought on  as the architect to help like navigate  the building code and zoning bylaws and  you know all the legal stuff and also to  help out on some of their more complex  projects and professionally i thought  this was a great move for me  because you know i could start to build  a niche in this area um especially  because most of my previous projects had  been smaller projects and renovations  this was partially motivated by my fear  of running out of time and dying  i'm gonna get to a point where i you  know need to have kids and have a family  and where i can't focus on my career as  much and so i always thought it's better  to start building a niche early so  that was my thinking at the time but the  more i was there i kept feeling like  maybe i'd picked a specialty a little  too early in my career i'd only been  working in the field for like three four  years which is like totally an infant in  this industry and ultimately i i felt  like this was a time for me to be like  trying out different projects of  different scales and be learning from  more senior architects so that was a  massive  miscalculation on my part and after  about half a year i ended up leaving  that firm which was really hard because  they'd kind of made this role for me  there and  i built really good relationships with a  lot of the people and i just ended up  disappointing a lot of people there but  again this was another time when i  really learned a lot about myself first  that i really don't like to disappoint  people and i really don't like conflict  but i think the biggest lesson from all  of this is that you kind of just have to  be ruthless about saying no to things  that don't really align with your goals  and you will make miscalculations and  you will disappoint people along the way  and i think you just need to be okay  with that it was the same with my  previous firm actually it was very hard  for me to leave because i developed all  these good relationships with these  people but you know if you know it's not  the right fit for you anymore and that  your goals don't really align with what  other people want for you  then it's not really in anyone's  interest to have you around you just  have to be your own cheerleader and make  those hard choices because nobody is  really gonna do that for you if you can  identify and seek out exactly what you  want and are okay with disappointing  people from time to time in the end  you're gonna be happier in all aspects  of life and you're gonna be surrounded  by people who are happier so anyways i'm  at a different firm now i'm super super  happy with the work i'm doing and my  goals are aligned with what the firm's  able to offer but  even after getting my license i still  feel like a newbie in this field so i'll  leave you with my final lesson which is  be an active  lifelong learner the human species is  biologically hardwired to evolve so  growth is the key to a satisfying life  when i was making the decision on  whether i should stay or leave my last  firm i was essentially  deciding between do i want to niche down  and develop this thing that i already  kind of have an expertise in or  do i want to jump into something more  foreign and learn to work with different  types of buildings there's no right or  wrong answer and your answer might  change depending on where you are in  life um and in your career or in your  financial situation but i think no  matter what situation you're in there's  